92186045585] [17592186124598 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360519 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124598 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360520 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124598 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360521 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124598 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360522 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124598 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124598 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124409 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124598 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125356 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125357 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125358 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125359 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125361 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125533 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1045 "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125356 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048558 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124715 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4535a3e9-8919-5e95-ab78-1b3e6bc5fe1d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125251 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406908 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125251 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94866" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125251 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125251 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125251 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cceedad6-c377-5d6a-a68c-beab3237c2c0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125251 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125356 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125533 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125356 #edb/ref 1061 17522 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125356 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b18f66cf-141c-5d9b-abc4-5dec05218f63" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125356 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125769 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125357 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125358 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125357 #edb/ref 1061 17523 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125357 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c37255e8-78c3-5483-a38d-706de2e319e3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125357 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124495 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125358 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125361 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125358 #edb/ref 1061 17524 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125358 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "914841e5-976c-50b0-9edd-a7475085a0ff" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125358 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125770 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125359 #edb/ref 1061 17525 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e0810ce-4ac6-549b-8bdf-98dce31cb769" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125771 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125361 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125359 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125361 #edb/ref 1061 17527 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125361 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e09fa444-e077-5819-b997-b79f912a52f2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125361 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124496 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125533 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125357 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125533 #edb/ref 1061 30897 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125533 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df7f4408-7a22-5728-a508-8a07da995a75" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125533 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124598 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125769 #edb/ref 1064 "A set of vectors \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}\n$$\n\nis called a **basis** of an abstract vector space $V$ if the following two conditions hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent, and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nFor example, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degree at most $2,$ and its subset \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{ {\\color{blue}{1}}, {\\color{blue}{t}}, {\\color{blue}{t^2}} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIs $\\mathcal{B}$ a basis for $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]?$ Let's check our two conditions.\n\n- First, we check for linear independence. Notice that the equation \n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} + x_2 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t} + x_3 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\n \nis true for all $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\nx_1=x_2=x_3=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$\n\n- Next, we check that $\\mathcal B$ spans $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$ Notice that we can write any polynomial $at^2+bt+c\\in\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ as a linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nat^2+bt+c=c\\cdot{\\color{blue}1} + b\\cdot{\\color{blue}t} + a\\cdot{\\color{blue}t^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal B$ spans $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {1}, {t}, {t^2} \\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125769 #edb/ref 1065 3210 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125769 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1d94c7bd-3777-5331-b633-0e2463092e26" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125769 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125770 #edb/ref 1064 "A set of vectors $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ is a basis of a vector space $V$ if $\\mathcal{B}$ is a **maximally linearly independent set in $V$**. In other words, $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent and for any vector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\in V,$ the set \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} \\cup \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\} = \\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\}\n$$\n\nis linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125770 #edb/ref 1065 3211 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125770 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dc0c1b8c-4abf-5ce9-aac8-763330c5a699" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125770 #edb/ref 1067 "An Alternative Definition" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125771 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ denote the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb{R}$ that have degree at most $n.$ Also, let $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ be the vector space of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$ Then, we have the following important standard bases.\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2 \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125771 #edb/ref 1065 3212 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125771 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fd76fc85-4c43-564f-96ac-8492b69f8ecf" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125771 #edb/ref 1067 "Some Important Standard Bases" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406915 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406916 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406917 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406918 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406919 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1045 "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186406915 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046679 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048558 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406880 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7a9a1a68-93b3-5d77-a9f3-56fa1ac555d7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406881 #edb/ref 1081 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406881 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406881 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "04483552-efe7-5ac0-9f49-b7cb22e5f5db" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406882 #edb/ref 1081 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406882 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [175