6887 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406893 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406894 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406896 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406903 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406909 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406909 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406909 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186406903 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406909 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "c77beab8-8132-549d-89ed-5a3840170386" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406910 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406882 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406910 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406886 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406910 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406895 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406910 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406898 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406910 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406899 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406910 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406910 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406910 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186406898 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406910 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "dc08e474-6dee-51de-a67a-78ce180dd388" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406911 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406906 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406911 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94942" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406911 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406911 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent\n- ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of $V$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406911 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d66ed66-f11f-53c8-af0e-1883d2bbdd40" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406911 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since if ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{2})+{x}_{3}(2{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{2}+(2{x}_{3}){b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3}(3{b}_{2})+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2}(2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406912 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406907 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406912 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94867" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406912 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406912 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406912 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "455cce4b-aebd-5209-87a1-ec6ed81f2e6b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406912 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & c \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406910 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406913 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107533" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406913 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406913 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b0d7b89-c3c8-5cee-b6a2-01b5cd1b1f67" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406913 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]=a\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-b)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-c)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+d\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406914 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406909 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406914 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80631" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406914 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406914 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ are bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406914 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3557614-de80-516a-bb07-7b50ae99a56a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406914 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2{b}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}(5{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2{x}_{1}){b}_{3}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+(5{x}_{3}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}(3{b}_{1})+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}(5{b}_{2})+(-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2})(-2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406915 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406918 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406915 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "748c2276-dafd-5926-8dff-6ce428e10c5e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406915 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406914 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406916 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406919 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406916 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aa1dd52d-ebd7-598e-b1e9-f1f6411b6354" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406916 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406911 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406917 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406916 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406917 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc7cf94a-8206-5fe0-b854-5c6affbe827a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406917 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406912 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406918 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406917 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406918 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8519c82-2015-5d4b-ab7d-68f0931d52be" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406918 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125251 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406919 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f5d3d190-875f-5a88-afc2-f8fcfd0bc6a4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406919 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406913 #edb/ref 17592186045585]}
